Every civic platform faces the same trap: it either demands that users abandon their existing networks and rebuild their audiences from zero, or it operates as a silo that no one outside the bubble ever sees.

PARA does neither. It is a second layer that sits on top of the networks people already use.

## How the second layer works

When you publish on PARA, your post carries civic context — #POLICY|| scores, #MATTER| tags, geographic markers, and RAQ positioning — and can sync to Bluesky, Twitter, and other open networks. Your audience stays where it is. Your message gains political legibility.

- Cross-network publishing preserves reach while adding civic structure.
- Shared political tags make posts readable as political speech, not generic content.
- No new audience to build. No platform to abandon.

## Trust infrastructure that scales

A layer is only as good as the trust beneath it. PARA combines two trust models through the same m8 proof broker:

- **Pseudonyms with cryptographic backing** — For users who need protection. Their expertise and participation carry weight without exposing their identity.
- **Verified identities** — For users and officials who need accountability. Their positions carry public credibility where that matters.

You choose your exposure level per post. The infrastructure supports both without forcing either.

## Why this matters

- Geographic trends become visible to parties, media, and researchers in real time.
- Civic participation scales without requiring civic courage.
- Anonymity technology serves broader public-sector value beyond any single app.

A trustworthy layer makes every voice count — not by making everyone shout louder, but by making speech structurally harder to ignore.
